    "narrative": "With a heavy focus on stolen base efficiency today, our strongest plays are anchored by elite speed profiles facing favorable pitching conditions. **James Wood** (76.28) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.98) headline the board; both benefit from matchups against pitchers with slow deliveries that maximize their stolen base upside. **James Wood** specifically sees his value bolstered by a +16% park factor and high xwOBA metrics, though he carries a 45% rain risk flag that requires monitoring. **Corbin Carroll** (74.38) remains a cornerstone, with the optimizer rewarding his top-tier dynamic platoon split against Nick Martinez despite a slight drag from the opponent's hold-runner capabilities. **Tyler Soderstrom** (67.58) also emerges as a high-value start, capitalizing on a favorable matchup against Walbert Ure\u00f1a to provide significant power production from the 4-hole.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Seiya Suzuki**, who projected at 45.43. While his order position (#3) provided a stability boost, the optimizer ultimately penalized him heavily due to the -19% SP skill adjustment against Jacob Misiorowski\u2014a high-end arm who significantly dampens his ceiling. Consequently, we opted for **Jose Caballero** (65.50) in the UTIL slot; despite his own 45% rain risk, his +13% dynamic platoon edge against Payton Tolle provided a much higher efficiency floor for our 5x5 categories. Similarly, **Alex Freeland** and **Cole Young** lingered in the 47-point range, but were kept on the bench as the optimizer prioritized the platoon-adjusted power and speed potential offered by our active starters. We are proceeding with caution on the weather-impacted games in the nation's capital, but the raw projected outputs for **Mickey Moniak** and **Corbin Carroll** are simply too robust to fade in our push for category dominance.",
